{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788312940,"nonce":"F9SuvfW56szfUkpVmiesvIW+f28=","title":"**Filed mid-run (m1-20260901, tick ~262,000). This one BLOCKS the run** — it is the dialog wedge `playbook/PLAY-LOOP.md` §Halts describes, arriving in a form 3.5's verbs cannot address.","message":"At tick ~255,000 the game raised `RimWorld.Dialog_NamePlayerFactionAndSettlement` — the prompt to name the player faction and its settlement. It is `forcePause` and `absorbsInput`, so every `advance` halts on it.\n\n`dialog-dismiss` reports success and removes the window:\n\n```\ndialog-dismiss -\u003e {\"dismissed\":[{\"type\":\"Dialog_NamePlayerFactionAndSettlement\",\"force_pause\":true,\"removed\":true}]}\n```\n\n…and the game raises it again **1,000 ticks later, every time.** Three consecutive turns, driven by the loop's own turn script:\n\n```\nwindows=Dialog_NamePlayerFactionAndSettlement  -\u003e dismissed -\u003e advance: {\"reason\":\"dialog\",\"ticks\":1000}\nwindows=Dialog_NamePlayerFactionAndSettlement  -\u003e dismissed -\u003e advance: {\"reason\":\"dialog\",\"ticks\":1000}\nwindows=Dialog_NamePlayerFactionAndSettlement  -\u003e dismissed -\u003e advance: {\"reason\":\"dialog\",\"ticks\":1000}\n```\n\nExactly 1,000 ticks each time, which says the trigger is a periodic check rather than a one-shot. With ~940,000 ticks left in the M1 run that is ~940 dismiss-and-halt cycles. The run cannot proceed on its own.\n\n\n- **`dialog-dismiss` closes without answering.** It removes the window; it does not satisfy the condition that raised it. For a one-shot dialog that is indistinguishable from answering — which is why this went unnoticed until now — and for a periodic one it is an infinite loop.\n- **`dialog-choose` requires a node tree.** Its own refusal path is explicit: it binds `Dialog_NodeTree.curNode` and returns `gate:\"no-readable-node\"` otherwise. `Dialog_NamePlayerFactionAndSettlement` is a **text-entry** window with no `DiaOption`s at all, so there is nothing for it to choose.\n- **`letter-choose` / `letter-dismiss`** are for the letter stack; this is a window, not a letter.\n\nSo the protocol has no verb that can put text into a text field, and this is the first shipped dialog that needs one.\n\n\n`interactions` already scrapes the window's instance fields, and **the answer is sitting in them, pre-filled and valid**:\n\n```json\n{\"type\":\"Dialog_NamePlayerFactionAndSettlement\",\n \"force_pause\":true,\"absorbs_input\":true,\"kind\":\"opaque\",\n \"fields\":{\"curName\":\"Enterean\",\n           \"curSecondName\":\"Gountown\",\n           \"nameMessageKey\":\"NamePlayerFactionMessage\",\n           \"invalidNameMessageKey\":\"PlayerFactionNameIsInvalid\",\n           \"secondNameMessageKey\":\"NamePlayerFactionBaseMessage_NameFactionContinuation\",\n           \"invalidSecondNameMessageKey\":\"PlayerFactionBaseNameIsInvalid\"},\n \"hint\":\"unrecognised window class; `fields` is a read-only scrape of its instance FIELDS\"}\n```\n\nThe game generated `Enterean` / `Gountown` itself and would accept them on OK. The verb does not need to invent a name or validate one — **accepting the window's own current value is a complete answer** for this dialog, and the two `invalid*MessageKey` fields name the validators if a caller ever wants to supply its own.\n\nNote that `interactions` labelling this an \"unrecognised window class\" is the honest half of the finding: the observation surface can see the window and read its state, and the action surface has no matching write.\n\n\n1. A verb can answer a text-entry force-pausing dialog by **accepting its current value** — the minimal, always-safe operation. Working name `dialog-accept`; it takes the window (by type or stack index, as `dialog-dismiss` does) and confirms rather than closes. `Dialog_NamePlayerFactionAndSettlement` is the case to prove it on: after one call, `interactions.force_pause.count` is 0 and stays 0 across a 60,000-tick advance.\n2. Optionally supplying text: `{text, second_text}` (or a generic `fields` map) validated through the window's own validator so a refusal is the game's refusal, not ours — the standing \"the gate lives in the widget\" rule. A rejected name comes back as `ok:false` with the game's `invalidNameMessageKey` reason, never silently.\n3. **`dialog-dismiss` stops claiming success on a dialog it did not answer.** Either it refuses on a window whose trigger will re-raise, or it reports what it did in terms a caller can branch on — the current envelope says `removed: true` and means \"for 1,000 ticks\". This is the same class as `253c694`'s silent order displacement: a truthful field that does not say the thing the caller needs.\n4. The `advance` halt path gets a guard for this shape: **N consecutive `dialog` halts on the same window type is a wedge**, not a series of events, and should be reported as one — `PLAY-LOOP.md`'s two-consecutive-0-tick rule does not catch it because each of these advances DOES run ticks (1,000 of them).\n5. `accept/\u003cid\u003e-dialog-accept.py` on a bench: raise the naming dialog, assert `dialog-dismiss` alone leaves it re-raising, assert the new verb clears it permanently, and assert a supplied invalid name is refused with the game's own message.\n6. `checklists/triggered.md`'s dialog handling and `PLAY-LOOP.md` §Halts both gain the text-entry case, with the escalation rule amended: a dialog the protocol cannot answer is escalated, and a dialog it can answer but only temporarily is worse than one it cannot, because it looks handled.\n\n\nEscalated to Evan, who is watching the bench window, to type the name by hand — recorded in the run report as human input beyond watching, since `664e9b9`'s invariants say there should be none. Without a fix this issue is a hard blocker for any unattended run that reaches the naming prompt, which every colony does.","files":null}]}